/**
 * @page clients USB/IP clients
 *
 * A program built on this library only *serves* a device: usbip_device_plug() listens on
 * TCP 3240 and nothing enumerates until the importing machine **attaches** it. The
 * importer is the USB/IP **client** - part of the OS, not of this library. (USB/IP
 * inverts the words: the side providing the device is the *server*; see the
 * @ref roles "role table".)
 *
 * @code{.text}
 *   your program                        the client (importer)
 *   USB device / USB-IP server   <---   USB host / USB-IP client   --->   OS USB stack
 *   listens :3240                       connects                          binds a driver
 * @endcode
 *
 * The attach commands differ per OS - one page each:
 *
 * Platform | Client | Attach with
 * ---------|--------|------------
 * @subpage cl_linux   | built in (`vhci-hcd`)   | `sudo usbip attach -r <ip> -b 1-1`
 * @subpage cl_windows | install `usbip-win2` / `usbip-win` | `usbip.exe attach -r <ip> -b 1-1`
 * @subpage cl_macos   | none in-box; one experimental one (SIP off) | a @ref cl_hw "hardware client", or drive from code
 * @subpage cl_hw      | importer firmware on a board | the board's own shell
 *
 * On **any** OS you can also skip the client entirely: the @ref host_api "host API"
 * imports and drives the device from your own process, with no kernel driver and no
 * root. That is how the test suite runs, and one of the few options on macOS.
 *
 * Whichever client you use: address the server as `127.0.0.1` when it is the same
 * machine, otherwise by IP - and then serve on `0.0.0.0` so it is reachable
 * (@ref tool_remote). The bus id is `1-1` unless you export
 * @ref dev_multi "several devices". Once attached, the OS binds its own in-box driver
 * and the device is indistinguishable from real hardware.
 *
 */
